# The Journey to Building a Web Server on a Hobby Operating System

In the world of technology, every so often, a passionate individual takes on the challenge of creating something entirely from scratch. One such inspiring endeavor is the development of a web server on a custom hobby operating system by an enthusiast known as joexbayer. This project not only showcases the deep understanding of networking and web protocols but also embodies the spirit of innovation and personal growth in the realm of computing.

## A Milestone Achieved

After a significant hiatus from coding, joexbayer returned to their project with renewed vigor, eventually achieving a crucial milestone: the successful implementation of a fully operational web server. The journey involved creating a comprehensive networking stack that included essential protocols such as Ethernet, IP, ARP, UDP, TCP, DHCP, and DNS. Each layer of this stack posed unique challenges, but with perseverance, the author refined the performance of TCP and perfected packet handling.

## Crafting the HTTP Engine

Building the networking stack is a commendable feat, but crafting an HTTP engine brings its own set of complexities. With HTTP being notoriously difficult to implement correctly, especially when dealing with the varied compliance of clients, joexbayer tackled the intricacies of text parsing and state management to create a robust HTTP engine from the ground up.

A snippet of the web server code highlights the simplicity yet effectiveness of the routing functionality:

```cpp
engine.get("/", [](const http::Request& req, http::Response& res) {
    (void)req;
    res.setBody("hello world!");
});
```

This small piece of code effectively demonstrates how requests can be handled seamlessly, returning a simple “hello world!” response—a classic first step for any budding web developer.

## Expanding Functionality

The web server built by joexbayer doesn't just stop at handling simple requests. It boasts routing capabilities that serve various files, like home and about pages, utilizing a file repository for efficient file management:

```cpp
webEngine.get("/home", [&fileRepo](const http::Request& req, http::Response& res) {
    (void)req;
    res.sendFile(fileRepo, "/web/index.htm");
});
```

The project is evolving with plans to implement a more sophisticated user interface and enhance features such as graceful shutdowns, showcasing the continuous growth mindset of its creator.

## Community Engagement

The journey has not only been a solitary endeavor; it has sparked conversations and engagement within the tech community. A thread on Hacker News surrounding this project highlights reactions from fellow developers and enthusiasts. Comments range from admiration for the engineering skills displayed to inquiries about the complexities faced during implementation, particularly in TCP and HTTP.

One commenter noted the intricacies of HTTP/1.1 compared to newer standards like HTTP/3, emphasizing the challenges of maintaining compliance with various clients. Such discussions reveal the collaborative nature of the tech community, where shared knowledge and experiences can guide and inspire others embarking on similar journeys.

